window.CARDS=(window.CARDS||[]).concat([{"t":"Root Cause Analysis","d":"Root Cause Analysis, also referred to as RCA, is a process of discovering the real problem and then taking remedial action to eliminate it.","g":2,"p":"Root Cause Analysis is a structured effort to find the underlying reason a problem occurs so the team can prevent it from returning.","e":"Example: A purchasing team investigates recurring failures in purchase-order approval. The team distinguishes the visible symptom from the underlying condition that must be corrected to prevent recurrence.","m":"Connect Root Cause Analysis to its practical purpose: what problem it reveals, prevents, measures, or improves.","x":"Do not confuse the definition of Root Cause Analysis with evidence that it has been applied correctly. Look for the required data, behavior, or decision.","r":["Core Principles in Root Cause Analysis","Five Whys Used in Root Cause Analysis","Root Cause Taxonomy"],"w":"Treating symptoms may provide temporary relief, but eliminating the underlying cause prevents recurrence."},{"t":"Core Principles in Root Cause Analysis","d":"Root cause analysis focuses on the underlying cause rather than symptoms, relies on evidence, considers the process as a system, and follows corrective actions through to verification.","g":2,"p":"Root cause analysis focuses on the underlying cause rather than symptoms, relies on evidence, considers the process as a system, and follows corrective actions through to verification.","e":"Example: A hospital clinic investigates recurring failures in patient check-in. The team distinguishes the visible symptom from the underlying condition that must be corrected to prevent recurrence.","m":"Connect Core Principles in Root Cause Analysis to its practical purpose: what problem it reveals, prevents, measures, or improves.","x":"Do not confuse the definition of Core Principles in Root Cause Analysis with evidence that it has been applied correctly. Look for the required data, behavior, or decision.","r":["Five Whys Used in Root Cause Analysis","Root Cause Analysis","Root Cause Taxonomy"],"w":"It helps prevent recurring failures by directing attention toward causes, risks, and controls rather than symptoms."},{"t":"Five Whys Used in Root Cause Analysis","d":"The 5 Whys is an interrogative and guided team approach that has proven very helpful when searching for the Root Cause.","g":2,"p":"The Five Whys repeatedly asks why a problem happened until the team reaches a cause that can be acted on. Five is a guideline, not a required number.","e":"Example: A laboratory finds repeated delays in sample processing. Instead of accepting “the request was incomplete,” the team uses the Five Whys until it traces the problem to unclear instructions at the start.","m":"Connect Five Whys Used in Root Cause Analysis to its practical purpose: what problem it reveals, prevents, measures, or improves.","x":"Do not stop at a convenient answer or assume the fifth answer is automatically the root cause. Verify causes with evidence.","r":["Core Principles in Root Cause Analysis","Root Cause Analysis","Root Cause Taxonomy"],"w":"It pushes the team beyond the immediate symptom toward a cause that can be prevented or controlled."},{"t":"The Five Whys: Six Steps","d":"A group is convened The problem is discussed and then clearly defined. It is then determined Why the problem occurred. The answer to this question then motivates a second Why. When the answer is given to the second WHY, the same question is asked again. Why? This is repeated until the question Why has been asked five times.","g":2,"p":"A group is convened The problem is discussed and then clearly defined.","e":"Example: A customer-support center finds repeated delays in ticket resolution. Instead of accepting “the request was incomplete,” the team uses the Five Whys until it traces the problem to unclear instructions at the start.","m":"Connect The Five Whys: Six Steps to its practical purpose: what problem it reveals, prevents, measures, or improves.","x":"Do not confuse the definition of The Five Whys: Six Steps with evidence that it has been applied correctly. Look for the required data, behavior, or decision.","r":["Five Whys Used in Root Cause Analysis","Scrum Sequence of Steps","Core Principles in Root Cause Analysis"],"w":"It helps reveal where work slows down, consumes resources, or fails to create customer value."},{"t":"Root Cause Taxonomy","d":"A root cause taxonomy organizes possible causes into categories such as design, equipment, material, human performance, management, communication, and training.","g":2,"p":"A root cause taxonomy organizes possible causes into categories such as design, equipment, material, human performance, management, communication, and training.","e":"Example: A warehouse investigates recurring failures in order fulfillment. The team distinguishes the visible symptom from the underlying condition that must be corrected to prevent recurrence.","m":"Connect Root Cause Taxonomy to its practical purpose: what problem it reveals, prevents, measures, or improves.","x":"Do not confuse the definition of Root Cause Taxonomy with evidence that it has been applied correctly. Look for the required data, behavior, or decision.","r":["Core Principles in Root Cause Analysis","Five Whys Used in Root Cause Analysis","Root Cause Analysis"],"w":"It helps prevent recurring failures by directing attention toward causes, risks, and controls rather than symptoms."},{"t":"Root Cause: Design","d":"The question is whether the design of the system is appropriate, whether implementation of the design has succeeded in meeting design standards, and whether that process has been validated to confirm its ability to deliver the results that are required.","g":2,"p":"This root-cause category asks whether the problem originated in design. The category is a prompt for investigation, not proof of the cause.","e":"Example: A software team investigates recurring failures in new-feature release. The team distinguishes the visible symptom from the underlying condition that must be corrected to prevent recurrence.","m":"Use Design as a cause category to investigate, then verify with data.","x":"A cause category is only a hypothesis. The team must verify that the suspected cause actually produces the problem.","r":["Core Principles in Root Cause Analysis","Five Whys Used in Root Cause Analysis","Root Cause Analysis"],"w":"It helps prevent recurring failures by directing attention toward causes, risks, and controls rather than symptoms."},{"t":"Root Cause: Equipment","d":"Are the settings correct? For example, are the readings correct? Are the readings correct over the useful range of the instrument or are there biases that could distort the accuracy and precision of those readings. Could these settings be the reason why the system is not performing or are there more serious problems tied to the equipment?","g":2,"p":"This root-cause category asks whether the problem originated in equipment. The category is a prompt for investigation, not proof of the cause.","e":"Example: A timer used by a manufacturer gives nearly identical readings for assembly process, but every reading is 20 seconds too high. The timer is precise but not accurate.","m":"Use Equipment as a cause category to investigate, then verify with data.","x":"A cause category is only a hypothesis. The team must verify that the suspected cause actually produces the problem.","r":["Core Principles in Root Cause Analysis","Five Whys Used in Root Cause Analysis","Root Cause Analysis"],"w":"It helps describe process performance accurately so teams do not make decisions from averages alone."},{"t":"Root Cause: Material","d":"Material can include items purchased from a supplier or items produced within the organization. In both cases some system of quality control must be established to ensure that the material inputs to the process are within acceptable ranges. When they are out of acceptable ranges this can be the source of a process problem.","g":2,"p":"This root-cause category asks whether the problem originated in material. The category is a prompt for investigation, not proof of the cause.","e":"Example: During one week, student registration at a university takes between 4 and 13 minutes. The range is 9 minutes, calculated as 13 minus 4.","m":"Use Material as a cause category to investigate, then verify with data.","x":"A cause category is only a hypothesis. The team must verify that the suspected cause actually produces the problem.","r":["Core Principles in Root Cause Analysis","Five Whys Used in Root Cause Analysis","Root Cause Analysis"],"w":"It keeps improvement connected to measurable customer needs instead of internal activity that may not create value."},{"t":"Root Cause: Human Performance","d":"Human performance is far from perfect. Failure to check work and simply pass unacceptable process outcomes to downstream operations is one example of failed human performance. But there are many other ways in human performance can contribute to a problem. For example, Sometimes the actions taken are not justified by the data.","g":2,"p":"This root-cause category asks whether the problem originated in human performance. The category is a prompt for investigation, not proof of the cause.","e":"Example: A restaurant investigates recurring failures in meal preparation. The team distinguishes the visible symptom from the underlying condition that must be corrected to prevent recurrence.","m":"Use Human Performance as a cause category to investigate, then verify with data.","x":"A cause category is only a hypothesis. The team must verify that the suspected cause actually produces the problem.","r":["Core Principles in Root Cause Analysis","Five Whys Used in Root Cause Analysis","Root Cause Analysis"],"w":"It helps prevent recurring failures by directing attention toward causes, risks, and controls rather than symptoms."},{"t":"Root Cause: Management","d":"The Root Cause can often be traced to management. Management policies or guidance may not be well defined, understood, or enforced.","g":2,"p":"This root-cause category asks whether the problem originated in management. The category is a prompt for investigation, not proof of the cause.","e":"Example: A bank investigates recurring failures in loan application. The team distinguishes the visible symptom from the underlying condition that must be corrected to prevent recurrence.","m":"Use Management as a cause category to investigate, then verify with data.","x":"A cause category is only a hypothesis. The team must verify that the suspected cause actually produces the problem.","r":["Core Principles in Root Cause Analysis","Five Whys Used in Root Cause Analysis","Root Cause Analysis"],"w":"It helps prevent recurring failures by directing attention toward causes, risks, and controls rather than symptoms."},{"t":"Root Cause: Communications","d":"Lack of effective communication can be the source of many problems. Research has concluded that one of the main reasons why projects fail is the lack of communication between management and those who are responsible for doing the work. But lack of communication and the problems it causes is not limited to those within the organization. It also extends to customer and suppliers","g":2,"p":"This root-cause category asks whether the problem originated in communications. The category is a prompt for investigation, not proof of the cause.","e":"Example: A airline investigates recurring failures in baggage handling. The team distinguishes the visible symptom from the underlying condition that must be corrected to prevent recurrence.","m":"Use Communications as a cause category to investigate, then verify with data.","x":"A cause category is only a hypothesis. The team must verify that the suspected cause actually produces the problem.","r":["Core Principles in Root Cause Analysis","Five Whys Used in Root Cause Analysis","Root Cause Analysis"],"w":"It keeps improvement connected to measurable customer needs instead of internal activity that may not create value."},{"t":"Root Cause: Training","d":"When problems occur, an important place to look is training. Has it been provided? Is it less than adequate? Then the results of the training sessions need to validated. Have the training goals been achieved?","g":2,"p":"This root-cause category asks whether the problem originated in training. The category is a prompt for investigation, not proof of the cause.","e":"Example: A pharmacy investigates recurring failures in prescription filling. The team distinguishes the visible symptom from the underlying condition that must be corrected to prevent recurrence.","m":"Use Training as a cause category to investigate, then verify with data.","x":"A cause category is only a hypothesis. The team must verify that the suspected cause actually produces the problem.","r":["Core Principles in Root Cause Analysis","Five Whys Used in Root Cause Analysis","Root Cause Analysis"],"w":"It helps prevent recurring failures by directing attention toward causes, risks, and controls rather than symptoms."},{"t":"Bar Charts","d":"A bar chart compares values across distinct categories using separate rectangular bars. The length or height of each bar represents the value for that category.","g":2,"p":"A bar chart compares values across distinct categories using separate rectangular bars.","e":"Example: During room turnover, a hotel keeps the steps the customer would recognize as useful and challenges steps that consume time without improving the outcome.","m":"Picture the visual first, then remember what decision the bar charts helps the team make.","x":"Choose the tool based on the question being answered. Similar-looking charts often serve different purposes.","r":["Bar Chart Displaying Mean and Variation","Root Cause: Communications","Root Cause: Training"],"w":"It turns process information into a visual pattern, helping the team recognize what deserves attention and choose an appropriate response."},{"t":"Pareto Principle","d":"Eighty percent of the consequences come from twenty percent of the causes. 80% of customer complaints can be traced to 20% of the customers. 80% of delays in a schedule originate in 20% of the project activities. 20% of the products and services contribute to 80% of the company’s profit. 20% of the sales force generates 80% of the sales.","g":2,"p":"The Pareto principle says a relatively small number of causes often account for a large share of the effect. It helps a team focus where improvement will matter most.","e":"Example: A delivery company ranks causes of problems in package routing and finds that two causes create most of the failures. The team tackles those high-impact causes first.","m":"Connect Pareto Principle to its practical purpose: what problem it reveals, prevents, measures, or improves.","x":"The 80/20 split is a useful pattern, not a universal mathematical law.","r":["Pareto Chart","Root Cause: Training","Bar Charts"],"w":"It keeps improvement connected to measurable customer needs instead of internal activity that may not create value."},{"t":"Pareto Chart","d":"A Pareto Chart is a variation of the bar chart. In addition to identifying the magnitude of the problem that each category represents, the Pareto Chart also displays the cumulative contribution associated with solving more than one problem.","g":2,"p":"A Pareto chart ranks categories from largest to smallest and usually adds a cumulative percentage line. It shows which causes contribute most to the problem.","e":"Example: A human-resources team ranks causes of problems in employee onboarding and finds that two causes create most of the failures. The team tackles those high-impact causes first.","m":"Bars descend from biggest to smallest so the vital few appear first.","x":"Choose the tool based on the question being answered. Similar-looking charts often serve different purposes.","r":["Pareto Principle","Bar Charts","Root Cause Analysis"],"w":"It directs limited improvement resources toward the causes producing the largest share of the problem."},{"t":"Fishbone Diagram","d":"A fishbone diagram is also referred to as a cause-and-effect diagram because it clearly connects the causes of a problem to the effects of that problem. It is a tool that visually decomposes or breaks down a process problem into successive layers, each one exposing more detail.","g":2,"p":"A fishbone diagram organizes possible causes into categories so the team can explore a problem broadly before testing which causes are real.","e":"Example: A finance team builds a fishbone diagram for errors in invoice processing, grouping possible causes under people, methods, equipment, materials, measurement, and environment.","m":"Picture the visual first, then remember what decision the fishbone diagram helps the team make.","x":"Choose the tool based on the question being answered. Similar-looking charts often serve different purposes.","r":["Root Cause Analysis","Five Whys Used in Root Cause Analysis","Root Cause Taxonomy"],"w":"It helps a team investigate broadly and avoid settling too quickly on the first plausible explanation."},{"t":"Three Fundamental Challenges","d":"Process improvement addresses three broad challenges: reducing delays, defects, errors, and waste; controlling output variation; and establishing monitoring and control.","g":2,"p":"Process improvement addresses three broad challenges: reducing delays, defects, errors, and waste; controlling output variation; and establishing monitoring and control.","e":"Example: A purchasing team reviews purchase-order approval and identifies waiting, duplicate entry, rework, and unnecessary handoffs. Removing those activities improves flow without reducing customer value.","m":"Connect Three Fundamental Challenges to its practical purpose: what problem it reveals, prevents, measures, or improves.","x":"Do not confuse the definition of Three Fundamental Challenges with evidence that it has been applied correctly. Look for the required data, behavior, or decision.","r":["Pareto Chart","Fishbone Diagram","Delays, Defects, Errors, and Waste"],"w":"It helps describe process performance accurately so teams do not make decisions from averages alone."},{"t":"Delays, Defects, Errors, and Waste","d":"This category is one of the three fundamental challenge of process improvement. It include the minimization of delays, defects, errors and waste.","g":2,"p":"This category is one of the three fundamental challenge of process improvement.","e":"Example: A hospital clinic reviews patient check-in and identifies waiting, duplicate entry, rework, and unnecessary handoffs. Removing those activities improves flow without reducing customer value.","m":"Connect Delays, Defects, Errors, and Waste to its practical purpose: what problem it reveals, prevents, measures, or improves.","x":"Do not confuse the definition of Delays, Defects, Errors, and Waste with evidence that it has been applied correctly. Look for the required data, behavior, or decision.","r":["Fishbone Diagram","Three Fundamental Challenges","Output Variation"],"w":"It helps prevent recurring failures by directing attention toward causes, risks, and controls rather than symptoms."},{"t":"Output Variation","d":"The second component of the three fundamental challenges of process improvement. This challenge is to minimize output variation such that one unit of output is very much the same as the previous and following units of outputs. In other words, output variation is managed and contained within specified limits.","g":2,"p":"The second component of the three fundamental challenges of process improvement.","e":"Example: A laboratory applies Output Variation while reviewing sample processing. The concept helps the team decide what evidence to collect, what action to take, and how to verify that performance improves.","m":"Variation answers: how different are the results, and is that difference routine or unusual?","x":"Do not confuse the definition of Output Variation with evidence that it has been applied correctly. Look for the required data, behavior, or decision.","r":["Bar Chart Displaying Mean and Variation","Common Cause Variation","Measures of Variation"],"w":"It helps describe process performance accurately so teams do not make decisions from averages alone."},{"t":"Monitoring and Control","d":"The third fundamental challenge is to establish monitoring and control systems that detect when a process is not performing as expected. Once this is detected, the system in analyzed to detect the root cause of the problem, changes are made, and the system brought back into control.","g":2,"p":"The third fundamental challenge is to establish monitoring and control systems that detect when a process is not performing as expected.","e":"Example: A customer-support center investigates recurring failures in ticket resolution. The team distinguishes the visible symptom from the underlying condition that must be corrected to prevent recurrence.","m":"Connect Monitoring and Control to its practical purpose: what problem it reveals, prevents, measures, or improves.","x":"Do not confuse the definition of Monitoring and Control with evidence that it has been applied correctly. Look for the required data, behavior, or decision.","r":["Delays, Defects, Errors, and Waste","Output Variation","X-Y Matrix Defined"],"w":"Improvement depends on behavior as well as tools. This concept helps people align, adopt new practices, and sustain results."},{"t":"X-Y Matrix Defined","d":"The X-Y Matrix, also called a Correlation Matrix, is a structured approach to ranking the importance of input variables. In this matrix, inputs and outputs are identified and then the impact of each of these inputs on the outputs is estimated.","g":2,"p":"An X-Y matrix ranks process inputs, the Xs, according to how strongly they affect important customer outputs, the Ys.","e":"Example: A warehouse applies X-Y Matrix Defined while reviewing order fulfillment. The concept helps the team decide what evidence to collect, what action to take, and how to verify that performance improves.","m":"Connect X-Y Matrix Defined to its practical purpose: what problem it reveals, prevents, measures, or improves.","x":"Do not confuse the definition of X-Y Matrix Defined with evidence that it has been applied correctly. Look for the required data, behavior, or decision.","r":["X-Y Matrix Illustrated","Project Defined (PMBOK)","Scrum Defined"],"w":"It gives the improvement team a practical way to understand, measure, or improve an important part of process performance."},{"t":"X-Y Matrix Illustrated","d":"An X-Y matrix lists process inputs against important outputs, weights the outputs by customer importance, and scores how strongly each input affects each output.","g":2,"p":"An X-Y matrix lists process inputs against important outputs, weights the outputs by customer importance, and scores how strongly each input affects each output.","e":"Example: A software team applies X-Y Matrix Illustrated while reviewing new-feature release. The concept helps the team decide what evidence to collect, what action to take, and how to verify that performance improves.","m":"Connect X-Y Matrix Illustrated to its practical purpose: what problem it reveals, prevents, measures, or improves.","x":"Focus on how to interpret the visual, not merely how it looks.","r":["X-Y Matrix Defined","Monitoring and Control","Project Defined (PMBOK)"],"w":"It keeps improvement connected to measurable customer needs instead of internal activity that may not create value."},{"t":"Project Defined (PMBOK)","d":"a sequence of related tasks that focus on an established objective. Projects are temporary in that they do not continue for an indefinite period of time.","g":2,"p":"A sequence of related tasks that focus on an established objective.","e":"Example: A manufacturer applies Project Defined (PMBOK) while reviewing assembly process. The concept helps the team decide what evidence to collect, what action to take, and how to verify that performance improves.","m":"Connect Project Defined (PMBOK) to its practical purpose: what problem it reveals, prevents, measures, or improves.","x":"Do not confuse the definition of Project Defined (PMBOK) with evidence that it has been applied correctly. Look for the required data, behavior, or decision.","r":["Components of a Project Plan","Scrum Defined","X-Y Matrix Defined"],"w":"It helps reveal where work slows down, consumes resources, or fails to create customer value."},{"t":"Components of a Project Plan","d":"A project plan normally defines the objective, scope, deliverables, tasks, schedule, resources, roles, risks, communication, and measures of success.","g":2,"p":"A project plan normally defines the objective, scope, deliverables, tasks, schedule, resources, roles, risks, communication, and measures of success.","e":"Example: A university applies Components of a Project Plan while reviewing student registration. The concept helps the team decide what evidence to collect, what action to take, and how to verify that performance improves.","m":"Connect Components of a Project Plan to its practical purpose: what problem it reveals, prevents, measures, or improves.","x":"Do not confuse the definition of Components of a Project Plan with evidence that it has been applied correctly. Look for the required data, behavior, or decision.","r":["Project Defined (PMBOK)","X-Y Matrix Illustrated","Scrum Defined"],"w":"It helps prevent recurring failures by directing attention toward causes, risks, and controls rather than symptoms."},{"t":"Scrum Defined","d":"Scrum is a project management framework that focuses on the details of project execution.","g":2,"p":"Scrum is an iterative way to manage complex work using short cycles, a prioritized backlog, defined roles, frequent review, and adaptation.","e":"Example: A restaurant applies Scrum Defined while reviewing meal preparation. The concept helps the team decide what evidence to collect, what action to take, and how to verify that performance improves.","m":"Connect Scrum Defined to its practical purpose: what problem it reveals, prevents, measures, or improves.","x":"Do not confuse the definition of Scrum Defined with evidence that it has been applied correctly. Look for the required data, behavior, or decision.","r":["Project Defined (PMBOK)","Scrum Process","Scrum Sequence of Steps"],"w":"Short feedback cycles help teams discover problems early and adapt priorities as they learn."},{"t":"Scrum Process","d":"Scrum organizes work into a prioritized product backlog and short sprints, with daily coordination, sprint reviews, and retrospectives used to inspect and adapt the work.","g":2,"p":"Scrum organizes work into a prioritized product backlog and short sprints, with daily coordination, sprint reviews, and retrospectives used to inspect and adapt the work.","e":"Example: A bank applies Scrum Process while reviewing loan application. The concept helps the team decide what evidence to collect, what action to take, and how to verify that performance improves.","m":"Connect Scrum Process to its practical purpose: what problem it reveals, prevents, measures, or improves.","x":"Do not confuse the definition of Scrum Process with evidence that it has been applied correctly. Look for the required data, behavior, or decision.","r":["Process Mean","Scrum Defined","Scrum Sequence of Steps"],"w":"It helps reveal where work slows down, consumes resources, or fails to create customer value."},{"t":"Scrum Sequence of Steps","d":"Vision Identification of features and requirements Organization of features and requirements into a product backlog Division of the Product Backlog into Sprints Execution of Sprints Monitoring and controlling of Sprints through Daily Scrum meetings Completion and review of the sprints Moving to the next sprint Completion of all Sprints and Sprint Retrospective","g":2,"p":"Vision Identification of features and requirements Organization of features and requirements into a product backlog Division of the Product Backlog into Sprints Execution of Sprints Monitoring and controlling of Sprints…","e":"Example: A airline applies Scrum Sequence of Steps while reviewing baggage handling. The concept helps the team decide what evidence to collect, what action to take, and how to verify that performance improves.","m":"Connect Scrum Sequence of Steps to its practical purpose: what problem it reveals, prevents, measures, or improves.","x":"Do not confuse the definition of Scrum Sequence of Steps with evidence that it has been applied correctly. Look for the required data, behavior, or decision.","r":["Scrum Defined","Scrum Process","The Five Whys: Six Steps"],"w":"It gives the improvement team a practical way to understand, measure, or improve an important part of process performance."},{"t":"Just-In-Time Processing","d":"Work does not begin on a product or service until a demand event occurs. Then, when it occurs, the inputs to the process – including raw materials, parts, or assemblies – are made available just as they are needed or ‘Just-In-Time.’","g":2,"p":"Just-in-Time means producing or delivering only what is needed, when it is needed, and in the amount needed. It reduces excess inventory but requires a dependable process.","e":"Example: A pharmacy releases materials for prescription filling only when downstream demand occurs, reducing excess inventory and storage while making reliable supply more important.","m":"Connect Just-In-Time Processing to its practical purpose: what problem it reveals, prevents, measures, or improves.","x":"Do not confuse the definition of Just-In-Time Processing with evidence that it has been applied correctly. Look for the required data, behavior, or decision.","r":["Scrum Process","Scrum Sequence of Steps","What is Kanban?"],"w":"Reducing excess inventory lowers cost and makes quality or flow problems visible sooner."},{"t":"What is Kanban?","d":"Kanban is framework for improving the efficiently of process scheduling. It matches a schedule to actual demand by pulling products or services through a process whenever demand occurs from a downstream source such as the customer. It does not push products through based on a schedule. Perhaps the most important lesson is that Kanban is a very useful tool for tracking workflow, gaining control of a process, and ensuring its efficiency.","g":2,"p":"Kanban is a visual pull system that signals when work or material should move. It limits work in progress and helps expose bottlenecks.","e":"Example: A hotel uses To Do, Doing, and Done columns for room turnover and limits the Doing column to five items. New work is pulled only when capacity opens.","m":"Connect What is Kanban? to its practical purpose: what problem it reveals, prevents, measures, or improves.","x":"Do not confuse the definition of What is Kanban? with evidence that it has been applied correctly. Look for the required data, behavior, or decision.","r":["Kanban Board","Kanban Example","Two-Bin Kanban"],"w":"Limiting work in progress exposes bottlenecks, reduces multitasking, and improves flow."},{"t":"Kanban Board","d":"To the right is a simple example of Kanban. It has three columns the TO DO column includes waiting to be processes. The DOING column represent job in progress. The DONE column includes those jobs that have been completed.","g":2,"p":"To the right is a simple example of Kanban.","e":"Example: A delivery company uses To Do, Doing, and Done columns for package routing and limits the Doing column to five items. New work is pulled only when capacity opens.","m":"Connect Kanban Board to its practical purpose: what problem it reveals, prevents, measures, or improves.","x":"Do not confuse the definition of Kanban Board with evidence that it has been applied correctly. Look for the required data, behavior, or decision.","r":["Kanban Example","Two-Bin Kanban","What is Kanban?"],"w":"It helps reveal where work slows down, consumes resources, or fails to create customer value."},{"t":"Two-Bin Kanban","d":"Users select items from Bin 1, the front bin. When Bin 1 is depleted, a replenishment order is sent to the supplier, and Bin 2 becomes the new Bin 1. When the replenishment order arrives, it becomes the new Bin 2.","g":2,"p":"Users select items from Bin 1, the front bin.","e":"Example: A human-resources team uses To Do, Doing, and Done columns for employee onboarding and limits the Doing column to five items. New work is pulled only when capacity opens.","m":"Connect Two-Bin Kanban to its practical purpose: what problem it reveals, prevents, measures, or improves.","x":"Do not confuse the definition of Two-Bin Kanban with evidence that it has been applied correctly. Look for the required data, behavior, or decision.","r":["Kanban Board","Kanban Example","Two Sources of Variation"],"w":"It gives the improvement team a practical way to understand, measure, or improve an important part of process performance."},{"t":"Kanban Example","d":"A Kanban system makes work visible, limits work in progress, and pulls a new item into the process only when capacity becomes available.","g":2,"p":"A Kanban system makes work visible, limits work in progress, and pulls a new item into the process only when capacity becomes available.","e":"Example: A finance team uses To Do, Doing, and Done columns for invoice processing and limits the Doing column to five items. New work is pulled only when capacity opens.","m":"Connect Kanban Example to its practical purpose: what problem it reveals, prevents, measures, or improves.","x":"Focus on how to interpret the visual, not merely how it looks.","r":["Kanban Board","Two-Bin Kanban","What is Kanban?"],"w":"It helps reveal where work slows down, consumes resources, or fails to create customer value."},{"t":"Process Mean","d":"The process mean is the average result associated with producing units of output from an operational process. It is called a measure of central tendency.","g":2,"p":"The process mean is the average result produced by a process. It describes the center, but not how widely results vary.","e":"Example: A purchasing team adds the completion times for 20 cases of purchase-order approval and divides by 20. The result is the process mean used as a measure of central tendency.","m":"Mean tells you where the center is; always ask what the variation looks like too.","x":"Do not confuse the definition of Process Mean with evidence that it has been applied correctly. Look for the required data, behavior, or decision.","r":["Bar Chart Displaying Mean and Variation","Scrum Process","Two-Bin Kanban"],"w":"It helps describe process performance accurately so teams do not make decisions from averages alone."},{"t":"Variation","d":"The degree to which data items in a dataset are dispersed from the mean","g":2,"p":"Variation is the natural difference among process results. Six Sigma tries to understand whether that difference is routine or caused by a specific event.","e":"Example: A hospital clinic adds the completion times for 20 cases of patient check-in and divides by 20. The result is the process mean used as a measure of central tendency.","m":"Variation answers: how different are the results, and is that difference routine or unusual?","x":"Do not confuse the definition of Variation with evidence that it has been applied correctly. Look for the required data, behavior, or decision.","r":["Bar Chart Displaying Mean and Variation","Common Cause Variation","Measures of Variation"],"w":"It helps describe process performance accurately so teams do not make decisions from averages alone."},{"t":"Measures of Variation","d":"Range and Standard Deviation","g":2,"p":"Range and Standard Deviation","e":"Example: Two laboratorys average the same completion time for sample processing, but one has results tightly grouped around the average. Its smaller standard deviation shows greater consistency.","m":"Variation answers: how different are the results, and is that difference routine or unusual?","x":"Do not confuse the definition of Measures of Variation with evidence that it has been applied correctly. Look for the required data, behavior, or decision.","r":["Bar Chart Displaying Mean and Variation","Common Cause Variation","Output Variation"],"w":"It helps describe process performance accurately so teams do not make decisions from averages alone."},{"t":"Range","d":"The range is the difference between the highest and lowest numbers in a dataset. The meteorologist tells us that the low temperature today will be 55 with a high of 75 degrees. The range is (75 - 55) 20 degrees.","g":2,"p":"The range is the largest value minus the smallest value. It is easy to calculate but uses only two observations.","e":"Example: During one week, ticket resolution at a customer-support center takes between 4 and 13 minutes. The range is 9 minutes, calculated as 13 minus 4.","m":"Connect Range to its practical purpose: what problem it reveals, prevents, measures, or improves.","x":"The range can change dramatically because of one extreme observation and ignores all values between the minimum and maximum.","r":["Variation","Measures of Variation","Standard Deviation"],"w":"It helps describe process performance accurately so teams do not make decisions from averages alone."},{"t":"Standard Deviation","d":"The standard deviation is a measure of variation that takes into consideration every item in a dataset, not just its extreme values as does the range.","g":2,"p":"Standard deviation measures how far values typically spread from the mean. A smaller standard deviation means the process is more consistent.","e":"Example: Two warehouses average the same completion time for order fulfillment, but one has results tightly grouped around the average. Its smaller standard deviation shows greater consistency.","m":"Connect Standard Deviation to its practical purpose: what problem it reveals, prevents, measures, or improves.","x":"Do not confuse the definition of Standard Deviation with evidence that it has been applied correctly. Look for the required data, behavior, or decision.","r":["Range","Variation","Normal Distribution"],"w":"Two processes can have the same average but very different consistency. Standard deviation exposes that difference."},{"t":"Standard Deviation Shortcut","d":"In this shortcut the standard deviation is equal to the range divide by six. If the range for call waiting times is 10 minutes, then an estimate of the standard deviation would be 10/6 = 1.66 minutes.","g":2,"p":"In this shortcut the standard deviation is equal to the range divide by six.","e":"Example: Two software teams average the same completion time for new-feature release, but one has results tightly grouped around the average. Its smaller standard deviation shows greater consistency.","m":"Connect Standard Deviation Shortcut to its practical purpose: what problem it reveals, prevents, measures, or improves.","x":"Do not confuse the definition of Standard Deviation Shortcut with evidence that it has been applied correctly. Look for the required data, behavior, or decision.","r":["Special Properties of Standard Deviation","Standard Deviation","Range"],"w":"It helps describe process performance accurately so teams do not make decisions from averages alone."},{"t":"Bar Chart Displaying Mean and Variation","d":"A bar chart can compare group averages while error bars or related measures show how much variation exists around each average.","g":2,"p":"A bar chart can compare group averages while error bars or related measures show how much variation exists around each average.","e":"Example: A manufacturer adds the completion times for 20 cases of assembly process and divides by 20. The result is the process mean used as a measure of central tendency.","m":"Picture the visual first, then remember what decision the bar chart displaying mean and variation helps the team make.","x":"Choose the tool based on the question being answered. Similar-looking charts often serve different purposes.","r":["Bar Charts","Common Cause Variation","Measures of Variation"],"w":"It turns process information into a visual pattern, helping the team recognize what deserves attention and choose an appropriate response."},{"t":"Histogram as a Continuous Distribution","d":"A histogram groups continuous numerical data into intervals so the shape, center, spread, and unusual patterns of the distribution can be seen.","g":2,"p":"A histogram groups numerical observations into intervals so the shape, center, spread, and unusual patterns of a distribution become visible.","e":"Example: A university groups student registration times into intervals on a histogram. The shape shows whether most cases cluster near the target or whether there is a long tail of slow cases.","m":"Connect Histogram as a Continuous Distribution to its practical purpose: what problem it reveals, prevents, measures, or improves.","x":"Do not confuse the definition of Histogram as a Continuous Distribution with evidence that it has been applied correctly. Look for the required data, behavior, or decision.","r":["Behavior of Normal Distribution","Normal Distribution","Plot of a Normal Distribution"],"w":"It helps describe process performance accurately so teams do not make decisions from averages alone."},{"t":"Two Sources of Variation","d":"Common and Special Cause","g":2,"p":"Common and Special Cause","e":"Example: A restaurant applies Two Sources of Variation while reviewing meal preparation. The concept helps the team decide what evidence to collect, what action to take, and how to verify that performance improves.","m":"Variation answers: how different are the results, and is that difference routine or unusual?","x":"Do not confuse the definition of Two Sources of Variation with evidence that it has been applied correctly. Look for the required data, behavior, or decision.","r":["Bar Chart Displaying Mean and Variation","Common Cause Variation","Measures of Variation"],"w":"It helps describe process performance accurately so teams do not make decisions from averages alone."},{"t":"Common Cause Variation","d":"Common Cause Variation represents the normal or expected random variation in a process. Not only is it inherent in any operational process, but it is predictable.","g":2,"p":"Common cause variation is the routine fluctuation built into the current process. Reducing it usually requires changing the process itself.","e":"Example: A bank applies Common Cause Variation while reviewing loan application. The concept helps the team decide what evidence to collect, what action to take, and how to verify that performance improves.","m":"Common cause is built into the common, everyday process.","x":"Do not confuse the definition of Common Cause Variation with evidence that it has been applied correctly. Look for the required data, behavior, or decision.","r":["Special Cause Variation","Bar Chart Displaying Mean and Variation","Core Principles in Root Cause Analysis"],"w":"It signals that the system itself must change rather than blaming a person or isolated event."},{"t":"Special Cause Variation","d":"Special Cause variation can be managed. It is the kind of variation that is not inherent in the process and, as a result, is not predictable. A process that is out-of-control is likely to produce results that are unacceptable. This process therefore is the source of Special Cause Variation.","g":2,"p":"Special cause variation comes from a specific, unusual source that is not normally part of the process. The first response is to investigate that event.","e":"Example: A airline applies Special Cause Variation while reviewing baggage handling. The concept helps the team decide what evidence to collect, what action to take, and how to verify that performance improves.","m":"Special cause comes from a special, identifiable event.","x":"Do not confuse the definition of Special Cause Variation with evidence that it has been applied correctly. Look for the required data, behavior, or decision.","r":["Common Cause Variation","Bar Chart Displaying Mean and Variation","Core Principles in Root Cause Analysis"],"w":"It points to an unusual source that should be investigated before redesigning the entire process."},{"t":"Multi-Vari Analysis","d":"In Multi-Vari analysis, a graph is created that displays the possible sources of variation affecting process output. It is a rather informal process and one that is very visual. No attempt is made to use statistics as a means of measuring the extent of the variation.","g":2,"p":"In Multi-Vari analysis, a graph is created that displays the possible sources of variation affecting process output.","e":"Example: A pharmacy adds the completion times for 20 cases of prescription filling and divides by 20. The result is the process mean used as a measure of central tendency.","m":"Connect Multi-Vari Analysis to its practical purpose: what problem it reveals, prevents, measures, or improves.","x":"Do not confuse the definition of Multi-Vari Analysis with evidence that it has been applied correctly. Look for the required data, behavior, or decision.","r":["Multi-Vari Chart","Core Principles in Root Cause Analysis","Five Whys Used in Root Cause Analysis"],"w":"It helps describe process performance accurately so teams do not make decisions from averages alone."},{"t":"Multi-Vari Chart","d":"A multi-vari chart visually separates variation within units, between units, and over time so the dominant source of variation can be identified.","g":2,"p":"A multi-vari chart visually separates variation within units, between units, and over time so the dominant source of variation can be identified.","e":"Example: A hotel applies Multi-Vari Chart while reviewing room turnover. The concept helps the team decide what evidence to collect, what action to take, and how to verify that performance improves.","m":"Picture the visual first, then remember what decision the multi-vari chart helps the team make.","x":"Choose the tool based on the question being answered. Similar-looking charts often serve different purposes.","r":["Multi-Vari Analysis","Bar Chart Displaying Mean and Variation","Pareto Chart"],"w":"It turns process information into a visual pattern, helping the team recognize what deserves attention and choose an appropriate response."},{"t":"Normal Distribution","d":"A distribution where the data items cluster toward the center and taper near the ends. One side is a mirror image of the other. Further, a normal distribution can be described by its mean and standard deviation. Shaped like a bell and often called a bell curve.","g":2,"p":"A normal distribution is a symmetric, bell-shaped pattern in which most observations are near the mean and fewer occur farther away.","e":"Example: Two delivery companys average the same completion time for package routing, but one has results tightly grouped around the average. Its smaller standard deviation shows greater consistency.","m":"Connect Normal Distribution to its practical purpose: what problem it reveals, prevents, measures, or improves.","x":"Do not confuse the definition of Normal Distribution with evidence that it has been applied correctly. Look for the required data, behavior, or decision.","r":["Behavior of Normal Distribution","Plot of a Normal Distribution","Histogram as a Continuous Distribution"],"w":"It helps describe process performance accurately so teams do not make decisions from averages alone."},{"t":"Plot of a Normal Distribution","d":"A normal distribution is a symmetric, bell-shaped curve centered on the mean, with its spread determined by the standard deviation.","g":2,"p":"A normal distribution is a symmetric, bell-shaped curve centered on the mean, with its spread determined by the standard deviation.","e":"Example: Two human-resources teams average the same completion time for employee onboarding, but one has results tightly grouped around the average. Its smaller standard deviation shows greater consistency.","m":"Picture the visual first, then remember what decision the plot of a normal distribution helps the team make.","x":"Focus on how to interpret the visual, not merely how it looks.","r":["Behavior of Normal Distribution","Normal Distribution","Histogram as a Continuous Distribution"],"w":"It turns process information into a visual pattern, helping the team recognize what deserves attention and choose an appropriate response."},{"t":"Behavior of Normal Distribution","d":"There are special properties of the normal distribution that permit us to make statements about the likelihood of data items or events falling within the range of the distribution. For example, 68.2 percent (34.1 + 34.1) of the data points or events that fall beneath this distribution will fall within plus or minus one standard deviation of the mean.","g":2,"p":"There are special properties of the normal distribution that permit us to make statements about the likelihood of data items or events falling within the range of the distribution.","e":"Example: Two finance teams average the same completion time for invoice processing, but one has results tightly grouped around the average. Its smaller standard deviation shows greater consistency.","m":"Connect Behavior of Normal Distribution to its practical purpose: what problem it reveals, prevents, measures, or improves.","x":"Do not confuse the definition of Behavior of Normal Distribution with evidence that it has been applied correctly. Look for the required data, behavior, or decision.","r":["Normal Distribution","Plot of a Normal Distribution","Histogram as a Continuous Distribution"],"w":"It helps describe process performance accurately so teams do not make decisions from averages alone."},{"t":"Special Properties of Standard Deviation","d":"For a normal distribution, about 68% of observations fall within one standard deviation of the mean, 95% within two, and 99.7% within three.","g":2,"p":"For a normal distribution, about 68% of observations fall within one standard deviation of the mean, 95% within two, and 99.7% within three.","e":"Example: Two purchasing teams average the same completion time for purchase-order approval, but one has results tightly grouped around the average. Its smaller standard deviation shows greater consistency.","m":"Connect Special Properties of Standard Deviation to its practical purpose: what problem it reveals, prevents, measures, or improves.","x":"Do not confuse the definition of Special Properties of Standard Deviation with evidence that it has been applied correctly. Look for the required data, behavior, or decision.","r":["Standard Deviation","Standard Deviation Shortcut","Special Cause Variation"],"w":"It helps describe process performance accurately so teams do not make decisions from averages alone."}]);
